{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The subdomain gap, and why the browser can't be the only enforcement point</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Never implement a state-changing action, anything that deletes, revokes, transfers, or updates something, so that it can be triggered by a plain <code>GET</code> request. This is true independent of SameSite entirely, it's the older and more fundamental rule that GET requests should be safe to fire without side effects, SameSite=Lax's carve-out for top-level GET is exactly why that older rule still matters today.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      SameSite's "same site" boundary is the registrable domain, so <code>uploads.victim.com</code> and <code>app.victim.com</code> are the same site to a cookie scoped there, even though they're different origins and may be operated by different, less-trusted teams. If either subdomain can be influenced by an attacker (unsanitized user-uploaded HTML served from it, a lower-security internal tool on it), that subdomain can issue same-site requests carrying the cookie, and SameSite offers zero protection against that path.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      SameSite is enforced entirely by the browser reading a cookie attribute, it verifies nothing server-side, and it isn't sent at all by non-browser HTTP clients or older WebView-based apps that don't implement the SameSite cookie-jar rules correctly. A server that has no CSRF-token check at all, relying purely on the client to have honored SameSite correctly, has no way to detect or reject a request from a client that simply didn't. Server-side token validation is the only half of this defense the server can actually verify happened.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

