  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">SameSite=Lax blocks a cookie from being sent on cross-site POST requests and subresource loads, but still sends it on a cross-site top-level GET navigation — a state change reachable via a plain link is not protected by SameSite at all.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Never implement a state-changing action behind GET. SameSite is scoped to specific request shapes, it can't retroactively protect an endpoint that was never inside the boundary it draws.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">SameSite's "same site" is the registrable domain, not the origin, so an untrusted subdomain is unprotected, and the browser-enforced attribute can't be verified server-side — a real CSRF token is still the only check the server can actually confirm happened.</div>
      </li>
